WEBVTT

00:00:15.880 --> 00:00:30.720
This is Coast!

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:37.160
As islanders, we're surrounded by the sea.

00:00:36.280 --> 00:00:42.880
It shapes and sustains us.

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:51.480
Beneath the waves lie watery riches, food and other treasures.

00:00:50.520 --> 00:01:00.720
Bounty that defines coastal living, and forges fruitful relationships across our seas.

00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:09.000
I'm heading for a unique place, a North Atlantic neighbour famous for its bounty.

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:11.360
Out there is somewhere I've never been, yet always wanted to.

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:21.000
People call it "nature's larder", and it's simply breathtaking.

00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:32.520
I'm touching down in the Faroe Islands.

00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:42.840
An archipelago of 18 islands, 200 miles north west of Shetland, the Faroe Islands are a self-governing nation within the Kingdom of Denmark.

00:01:41.960 --> 00:01:48.760
While I'm in lands afar, the rest of the team are casting off on their own search for bounty.

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:58.080
Mark's venturing into unknown waters in the wake of our earliest fishermen.

00:01:57.240 --> 00:01:58.920
We're trying to fish, the boat is burning...

00:01:57.960 --> 00:02:02.400
This is such a risky operation!

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:11.360
Hermione's discovering how a microscopic bounty aroused passions in Victorian art and science.

00:02:10.639 --> 00:02:17.520
Plankton opened up a whole new world of mystery, of the magic of nature.

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:24.000
Tess is finding out how help from the seashore was enlisted to keep Britain flying during the Second World War.

00:02:23.320 --> 00:02:32.800
A German scientist stepped in with a unique solution, he would save our skies with seaweed.

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:40.280
And I'm searching up top and down deep for an array of Faroese fodder!

00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:46.840
This is bounty from the sea.

00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:15.120
I'm in the Faroe Islands, a place totally dependent on the sea and its riches.

00:03:14.680 --> 00:03:24.400
Out there is a world of opportunity, oceans awash with bounty, a natural larder.

00:03:23.480 --> 00:03:30.920
I want to discover what makes the Faroe Islands so reliant on the sea.

00:03:29.960 --> 00:03:31.240
What makes this coast and these waters so fertile?

00:03:31.320 --> 00:03:36.600
To find out, I'm embarking on an island odyssey.

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:57.320
My journey will take me around the islands, starting in the capital, Torshavn, I'm making my way to Muli on Bordoy, then to Gjogv on Eysturoy where I'll attend a Faroese gathering - including a feast.

00:03:56.440 --> 00:04:03.160
I might be miles from home, but Britain has long been linked to this place by bounty.

00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:17.480
The British supplied the first fishing smack in the 1850s that took the Faroese fishing fleet offshore, creating a population explosion and changing this archipelago forever.

00:04:16.760 --> 00:04:24.800
Today, the Faroe Islands are one of the six biggest suppliers of fish into the UK.

00:04:23.880 --> 00:04:30.280
An astonishing 90% of the Faroese export is fish.

00:04:29.440 --> 00:04:39.360
Flourishing North Atlantic waters are home to species such as cod, herring, mackerel and salmon.

00:04:38.480 --> 00:04:48.800
Bounty from these seas defines this isolated community and surviving the harsh winter months means nothing is off-limits.

00:04:48.040 --> 00:04:55.280
For 400 years, the Grindadrap whale hunt has been a ritual which continues today.

00:04:54.520 --> 00:05:00.680
This custom is controversial and draws objections from the wider world.

00:05:01.360 --> 00:05:11.240
Gathering food for the table is a tradition handed down through the generations here.

00:05:10.360 --> 00:05:20.520
Be it on land or in the sea, I want to unlock the secrets of these bountiful waters by doing some Faroese foraging of my own.

00:05:19.720 --> 00:05:29.160
I'll see what I can bring to the table at the end of my journey.

00:05:28.360 --> 00:05:33.280
But what, beyond fish, thrives in these waters.

00:05:32.320 --> 00:05:44.800
Birgir Enni, a local diver and lifelong underwater forager, is taking me in search of other edible treasures.

00:05:45.680 --> 00:05:47.600
Hello, Birgir!

00:05:46.640 --> 00:05:50.320
We're sailing north out of the main town of Torshavn, the capital of this little archipelago.

00:05:51.080 --> 00:06:02.920
I'm aboard the Nordslyd, a traditional Faroese schooner, built here in the 1940s.

00:06:04.160 --> 00:06:07.080
What are we looking for, Birgir?

00:06:06.240 --> 00:06:08.360
The mussels, the horse mussels.

00:06:07.560 --> 00:06:12.520
And where do we find Faroese horse mussels?

00:06:11.600 --> 00:06:15.480
Yeah, all around the island you can find them.

00:06:14.600 --> 00:06:15.840
Yeah, in the Fjord especially.

00:06:17.480 --> 00:06:22.760
Back home, we predominantly eat the blue mussel.

00:06:21.920 --> 00:06:27.920
Here, on the Faroes, appetites are somewhat bigger.

00:06:27.200 --> 00:06:30.440
This is one from the British water.

00:06:29.680 --> 00:06:32.320
OK, I'm familiar with these.

00:06:31.880 --> 00:06:35.880
I have one here, this is, this is our mussels.

00:06:35.480 --> 00:06:36.880
That's enormous!

00:06:36.080 --> 00:06:39.360
It's like a small boat.

00:06:38.800 --> 00:06:39.920
Yeah, yeah.

00:06:39.040 --> 00:06:43.920
They can be up to 45-years-old.

00:06:43.040 --> 00:06:45.280
They grow very, very slowly.

00:06:44.760 --> 00:06:47.920
They are full of meat - I like them best of all.

00:06:47.080 --> 00:06:51.320
It's for health, fire food - it makes you strong.

00:06:52.840 --> 00:06:59.680
Finding these monsters from the deep means taking the plunge and getting wet.

00:06:58.880 --> 00:07:00.880
This is a Faroese swimming costume.

00:07:01.800 --> 00:07:07.960
As Birgir dives into the depths, I'm charged with searching closer to shore.

00:07:08.320 --> 00:07:16.240
But I'm the world's worst snorkeler, so the chances of me finding anything are slight.

00:07:26.960 --> 00:07:33.920
Despite its northerly latitude, the water is surprisingly temperate.

00:07:32.960 --> 00:07:46.120
That's because the Faroe Islands sit right in the path of the Gulf Stream - a warm Atlantic current that brings nutrient-rich waters from the Gulf of Mexico right up into the North Atlantic.

00:07:45.400 --> 00:07:56.400
It makes for waters that never drop below six or seven degrees all year round.

00:07:55.720 --> 00:08:04.600
Such clear conditions are courtesy of strong currents and little heavy industry.

00:08:03.640 --> 00:08:12.120
Coupled with a steady temperature and rich supply of plankton, it's the perfect habitat for horse mussels.

00:08:11.160 --> 00:08:19.000
These sleeping giants can lie undisturbed for decades, growing and growing.

00:08:18.240 --> 00:08:27.840
Living part-buried in the sediment and amongst all this kelp, they're not an easy bounty to spot.

00:08:27.040 --> 00:08:28.760
But Birgir has come up trumps!

00:08:30.080 --> 00:08:32.600
Look what Birgir has found.

00:08:42.080 --> 00:08:45.159
Look at the size of them!

00:08:44.320 --> 00:08:46.560
Birgir, how old do you think this one is?

00:08:45.640 --> 00:08:46.800
Yeah.

00:08:45.960 --> 00:08:48.960
30 years, 30 years..

00:08:48.000 --> 00:08:51.440
30 years it's been growing just here.

00:08:51.680 --> 00:08:52.840
Yes.

00:08:51.880 --> 00:08:56.120
This is the first contribution to my Faroese feast.

00:08:55.160 --> 00:08:56.480
Look at these.

00:08:59.880 --> 00:09:02.600
You are very welcome.

00:09:01.840 --> 00:09:04.440
Welcome to our garden.

00:09:05.880 --> 00:09:12.800
Surviving on the coast requires ingenuity and resolve.

00:09:11.880 --> 00:09:17.560
It characterises its people who make a life on the edge.

00:09:16.840 --> 00:09:22.920
Our seas promise rich pickings if we can harvest them.

00:09:24.440 --> 00:09:38.280
From the fruitful North Atlantic to the fish-rich North Sea, fishing has united coastal communities for thousands of years.

00:09:37.480 --> 00:09:42.240
And this shared heritage stretches back to a time when this sea didn't even exist.

00:09:43.240 --> 00:09:56.680
Mark's heading for Ertebolle on the north west coast of Denmark to investigate archaeological evidence of the very first Europeans to fish at sea.

00:10:01.240 --> 00:10:12.400
7,000 years ago in the Mesolithic period, this fjord would have been a lush, bountiful landscape.

00:10:11.640 --> 00:10:24.240
Now flooded, these waters hold the secret to a Stone Age technology that would forever change our coast.

00:10:23.600 --> 00:10:37.920
In the 1970s, archaeologists made a remarkable discovery, some three metres down on the seabed they found a perfectly preserved Stone Age village.

00:10:37.080 --> 00:10:46.960
It would transform our understanding of how people lived in the past.

00:10:46.440 --> 00:10:53.760
I might be in Denmark, but our two coasts were once linked.

00:10:52.800 --> 00:10:54.240
There's the mountains of Scotland.

00:10:53.280 --> 00:11:02.160
That's because a landmass used to connect Britain and mainland Europe.

00:11:01.360 --> 00:11:02.960
So, let's put seaweed in, which was once dry land.

00:11:02.040 --> 00:11:06.160
It's known as Doggerland.

00:11:05.400 --> 00:11:14.840
Over the centuries, sea levels rose and flat flood plains were reclaimed by the North Sea.

00:11:14.280 --> 00:11:31.320
People were forced to choose which shrinking landmass to follow, one towards Britain or one towards mainland Europe.'

00:11:34.360 --> 00:11:52.600
Soren Andersen was a principal investigator at the excavation of Tybrind Vig, a 7,000-year-old settlement found submerged in a fjord.

00:11:51.760 --> 00:12:01.960
Discoveries included pots, tools and graves, but there was something more surprising - three boats.

00:12:02.640 --> 00:12:06.760
And I have a slide here where you can see one.

00:12:06.160 --> 00:12:12.520
That's a big one lying on the dry land, on the beach, for the first time in 6,500 years!

00:12:11.600 --> 00:12:14.680
I mean, that must be some of the oldest boats in Europe.

00:12:16.200 --> 00:12:17.680
It is, it is.

00:12:16.840 --> 00:12:22.080
And what surprised us mostly, it was ten metres long.

00:12:21.600 --> 00:12:30.040
So, it's an enormous trunk has been used for that boat.

00:12:29.800 --> 00:12:38.080
The boat was proof of a shifting way of life from hunting on land to hunting at sea.

00:12:37.360 --> 00:12:41.440
Prior to this, Stone Age people were transient hunter-gatherers.

00:12:40.600 --> 00:12:48.640
By building the first boats, they could now fish from the sea and settle on the coast.

00:12:47.800 --> 00:12:55.760
The local museum has built a replica of one of the original boats, which contained an unexpected feature.

00:12:56.040 --> 00:13:03.360
What is surprising is that we have had a fireplace or hearth to the rear of the boat.

00:13:02.440 --> 00:13:03.560
I mean, it's a completely mad idea, isn't it?

00:13:02.600 --> 00:13:05.560
Lighting a fire in a wooden boat?

00:13:05.400 --> 00:13:08.280
They had a fire in a wooden boat.

00:13:07.520 --> 00:13:13.160
It was a discovery that confounded archaeologists.

00:13:12.240 --> 00:13:23.040
Not only had they found evidence the first fishing boats, 7,000 years ago, Stone Age people were taking fire out to sea.

00:13:22.200 --> 00:13:24.520
What was going on?

00:13:25.920 --> 00:13:33.560
This might look like an empty field, but it could unlock the answer.

00:13:32.720 --> 00:13:41.440
In the 1890s, a huge Stone Age rubbish dump or midden was uncovered here.

00:13:40.680 --> 00:13:51.440
It dated to the same period as the boats and it was full of millions of shells and fish bones.

00:13:50.680 --> 00:13:53.160
Look!

00:13:52.280 --> 00:13:53.520
Here we've got some of the shells that have been thrown up by animals.

00:13:52.920 --> 00:13:59.120
Oyster and scallops and all sorts of things here.

00:13:58.160 --> 00:14:10.000
When the midden was originally excavated, it produced many really important finds, including this extraordinary, exquisite fish hook from the Stone Age.

00:14:09.040 --> 00:14:23.240
This coastal bounty, masses of bones from protein-rich fish together with the boats, suggests these people were prolific and successful fishermen.

00:14:22.960 --> 00:14:29.480
Among the bones found in the midden, were a number of species that were typically caught at night.

00:14:28.640 --> 00:14:34.480
Could that perhaps be the reason for the fires on the boats?

00:14:34.080 --> 00:14:41.320
Eel bones were found in their thousands among the Stone Age rubbish.

00:14:41.480 --> 00:14:49.280
These are fish most active after dark, as retired fisherman Enjar Grevy remembers.

00:14:49.920 --> 00:14:52.840
Can I come on-board?

00:14:52.080 --> 00:14:53.360
Yes.

00:14:52.480 --> 00:15:00.640
Enjar fished for the elusive eel at night using lamp light, a practice now banned.

00:14:59.760 --> 00:15:03.360
Did that attract the eels?

00:15:02.520 --> 00:15:04.720
Yes.

00:15:03.880 --> 00:15:13.640
Where the lights end, you can see the eels come slowly passing by.

00:15:12.680 --> 00:15:13.920
Sometimes just opposite the boat.

00:15:14.640 --> 00:15:19.120
When the light was on and I saw the eel...

00:15:19.120 --> 00:15:20.200
Very slowly, down... ding-a-ling-ling... ..then pull them in.

00:15:24.200 --> 00:15:27.320
As easy as that?

00:15:26.400 --> 00:15:27.760
Yes.

00:15:30.320 --> 00:15:44.240
The idea that this type of fishing might have origins dating back over 7,000 years on this fjord, is something I want to test.

00:15:43.560 --> 00:15:59.520
We've been given special permission by the government to do some experimental archaeology to find out how and why our Stone Age ancestors might have fished at night.

00:15:58.760 --> 00:16:05.920
Stone Age expert Jacqui Wood is helping me.

00:16:06.400 --> 00:16:08.320
That should be enough.

00:16:07.400 --> 00:16:08.560
You reckon so?

00:16:07.720 --> 00:16:09.000
Yeah.

00:16:08.120 --> 00:16:10.520
Right!

00:16:13.040 --> 00:16:15.280
In it goes.

00:16:14.600 --> 00:16:25.960
Findings from the Stone Age boats revealed that embers sat on a bed of sand and clay to protect the wood from burning.

00:16:25.040 --> 00:16:32.560
Leave that to settle a little bit and we'll take some logs with us to keep it going.

00:16:31.720 --> 00:16:35.880
Off we go!

00:16:37.840 --> 00:16:47.080
Jacqui and assistant Kif have brought along a prehistoric fishing kit of harpoons and flares, all based on archaeological findings.

00:16:48.040 --> 00:16:53.360
While Stone Age man would have known what to do, for us it's more error than trial, and it's not long before we're in trouble.

00:16:52.520 --> 00:16:54.080
Hang on we've got a problem here, Jacqui.

00:16:56.040 --> 00:16:57.160
OK.

00:16:56.440 --> 00:17:02.480
I've dumped a whole lot of bark at the back there...

00:17:01.600 --> 00:17:02.880
No!

00:17:02.040 --> 00:17:04.160
OK, well...

00:17:03.360 --> 00:17:07.119
This is completely mad, isn't it?

00:17:06.240 --> 00:17:12.400
We're in the middle of the North Sea, in a dug-out canoe.

00:17:11.640 --> 00:17:16.640
I mean, we're trying to fish, the boat is burning - this is such a risky operation.

00:17:15.920 --> 00:17:24.040
But it must have been a really sort of profitable one to the people in the Mesolithic, or they wouldn't have done it, would they?

00:17:23.160 --> 00:17:28.599
You either drown or you starve.

00:17:27.720 --> 00:17:33.640
With the fire under control, the fishing can get underway.

00:17:34.240 --> 00:17:42.920
Jacqui believes our ancestors used bundles of birch bark slotted into sticks as flares.

00:17:42.160 --> 00:17:44.920
It's amazing how deep you can see into the water.

00:17:44.360 --> 00:17:50.160
Fire had a dual purpose, to light the way and lure the fish.

00:17:49.320 --> 00:18:00.840
It appears that light fools the fish into thinking it's a different time of day, or they mistake it for luminescent prey.

00:17:59.880 --> 00:18:01.120
Isn't that beautiful?

00:18:00.240 --> 00:18:02.600
Isn't that amazing?

00:18:01.640 --> 00:18:06.160
It's just like tea lights, isn't it?

00:18:05.320 --> 00:18:08.880
They're like little tar boats.

00:18:08.040 --> 00:18:18.640
Tar, found in the birch bark, acts as an accelerant, creating brilliant candles that burn on the water.

00:18:17.960 --> 00:18:19.280
Do you think they knew about this bark and this extraordinary characteristic of it?

00:18:18.400 --> 00:18:19.600
Oh, absolutely.

00:18:18.680 --> 00:18:24.880
Because they actually cooked the bark underground in pits with fires to get the actual tar off.

00:18:25.400 --> 00:18:27.400
So, there it is.

00:18:27.320 --> 00:18:29.440
It's burning still on the surface.

00:18:31.400 --> 00:18:32.600
Like little candles, aren't they?

00:18:31.680 --> 00:18:36.640
You can see how that, that will actually, that light will then go through the water...

00:18:35.920 --> 00:18:37.240
Yep.

00:18:36.360 --> 00:18:41.120
..And really attract the eel.

00:18:41.880 --> 00:18:47.360
So, basically this would fascinate the eels and they'd come to see what it is and then we'd actually harpoon them.

00:18:46.680 --> 00:18:48.360
There's something down there, actually.

00:18:50.840 --> 00:18:56.960
My harpoon with a bone point tip is designed for trapping eels.

00:18:56.160 --> 00:19:00.120
But I'm not having much luck.

00:18:59.240 --> 00:19:03.280
I don't think I'd make a very good Mesolithic fisherman!

00:19:06.680 --> 00:19:13.480
We haven't matched the skills of our Stone Age ancestors and with eel numbers down and wind conditions less than favourable, we leave empty handed.

00:19:13.640 --> 00:19:17.200
But we've proved the principle that you can take fire to sea and fish at night.

00:19:19.640 --> 00:19:32.560
After this experiment, I'm completely convinced that Stone Age people used lights to fish at night here on this fjord.

00:19:31.840 --> 00:19:39.200
A practice they started continued for thousands of years.

00:19:38.360 --> 00:19:47.320
Our forebears were some of the first boat builders and advanced fishermen.

00:19:46.440 --> 00:19:48.000
Pioneers in coastal living.

00:19:47.480 --> 00:19:56.120
Their ingenuity enabled them to exploit the incredible bounty that lay underneath the sea.

00:20:04.080 --> 00:20:11.840
Our Stone Age ancestors fished to survive.

00:20:11.080 --> 00:20:18.640
The bounty they ate was one they caught themselves from unsullied seas.

00:20:17.720 --> 00:20:23.040
Today, our world is one of cleaned, processed, pre-packed bounty.

00:20:22.440 --> 00:20:29.320
And some of what we take out of our waters is wrapped in a problem that goes back in - plastic.

00:20:30.600 --> 00:20:50.600
A billion tonnes has been discarded since the '50s, a floating coastal scrapheap, which may take thousands of years to degrade.

00:20:50.920 --> 00:21:01.960
In Orford Ness, on the east coast of England, artist Fran Crowe is on a mission to turn one person's rubbish into another's bounty.

00:21:03.240 --> 00:21:15.080
I came across a United Nations report that estimated on average there are 46,000 pieces of plastic floating on every square mile of sea.

00:21:14.200 --> 00:21:27.520
And in a rather mad, crazy moment I decided to set myself a challenge of saving one square mile of sea by collecting 46,000 plastic pieces whilst walking my local beaches.

00:21:26.560 --> 00:21:30.280
It took me a year to do.

00:21:31.040 --> 00:21:36.360
On a typical walk, I'd find maybe 700 pieces, even on a beach that looked pristine when you went there.

00:21:36.040 --> 00:21:44.280
And on Orford Ness, I could probably pick up several thousand, but I couldn't actually carry everything I saw.

00:21:43.440 --> 00:21:53.640
When I get the stuff home, first of all I have to dry it, get the sand off it and so on, but then I start to sort it.

00:21:52.760 --> 00:22:00.240
And I'll sort it by colour, for instance, putting all the pink and red plastics together or the blue.

00:22:00.800 --> 00:22:07.720
I also sort it by type, so I'm putting bottle tops together, I've got cotton bud sticks here.

00:22:06.800 --> 00:22:08.360
These have definitely been flushed from homes.

00:22:07.720 --> 00:22:16.080
It's the materials for my art and it's my personal bounty from the sea.

00:22:15.360 --> 00:22:19.120
I am making something beautiful with them often.

00:22:22.040 --> 00:22:32.360
That isn't my main purpose, but I do hope that what I'm doing will inspire people to think differently about these kind of objects and how we use them.

00:22:34.840 --> 00:22:45.280
Today, what I've collected from this tiny bit of Orford Ness, I'm going to just put into one big pile.

00:22:45.320 --> 00:22:54.160
This is going to be... a seafood soup, harvested by hand from the North Sea.

00:22:53.320 --> 00:22:57.720
To serve, just add water and salt.

00:23:06.520 --> 00:23:18.360
I'm 200 miles offshore from Britain in the Faroe Islands.

00:23:17.840 --> 00:23:21.440
Here, you're never more than three miles from the sea.

00:23:20.680 --> 00:23:29.600
It's made the Faroese experts in coastal living, reliant on, and defined by, bounty from these waters.

00:23:29.720 --> 00:23:38.680
I'm on a quest to find out how they survive in the middle of the North Atlantic.

00:23:37.800 --> 00:23:47.560
I've already bagged, with a little bit of help, some giant mussels from the deep, now I want to unearth whatever delicacies lurk on land.

00:23:46.680 --> 00:23:55.200
It might look green, but this treeless coast is battered by the elements.

00:23:54.400 --> 00:24:03.920
I want to know what can thrive in these conditions and how.

00:24:03.400 --> 00:24:13.440
The Faroes' position in the path of the Gulf Stream creates verdant waters, rich in bounty, but things are tougher on land.

00:24:12.600 --> 00:24:24.360
Four seasons in one day is the norm and being a tiny archipelago in a vast ocean means growing anything is a struggle.

00:24:24.800 --> 00:24:26.680
So the locals have learned to harvest other things.

00:24:25.840 --> 00:24:37.320
Sea birds flock to towering cliffs, guillemots, scavenging great skuas, parading puffins.

00:24:36.360 --> 00:24:50.080
Past and present, the Faroese risk life and limb, swinging on a wing and a prayer to net this feathered quarry and gather their eggs.

00:24:58.200 --> 00:25:04.880
But when it comes to greens, there's a four-legged competitor for these scarce riches.

00:25:06.800 --> 00:25:08.440
Sheep.

00:25:07.720 --> 00:25:11.360
And they're everywhere.

00:25:10.680 --> 00:25:24.120
Already here when the Vikings, the first documented settlers, arrived 1,000 years ago, they gave this place its name - The Faroes or Land Of The Sheep.

00:25:23.360 --> 00:25:29.360
Today, there are more of them on the islands than there are people.

00:25:28.600 --> 00:25:36.200
As a result, mutton, like fish, forms a large part of the Faroese diet.

00:25:37.920 --> 00:25:39.080
But here it doesn't come in its freshest form.

00:25:38.240 --> 00:25:45.640
It's called skerpikjot and it's dried outside in ocean winds, another bounty from these seas.

00:25:47.200 --> 00:25:54.640
I'm heading for the tiny hamlet of Muli on Bordoy to meet shepherd Archie Black.

00:25:54.720 --> 00:25:57.560
Hello, nice meeting you.

00:25:57.280 --> 00:26:01.840
He's going to introduce me to this peculiar Faroese delicacy.

00:26:01.080 --> 00:26:04.320
What do you call this little house?

00:26:04.920 --> 00:26:09.840
This is wind drying house for sheep meat.

00:26:09.000 --> 00:26:11.240
So, this is the meat here hanging up drying, is it?

00:26:10.280 --> 00:26:15.120
Yes, it's the back legs of the sheep.

00:26:14.880 --> 00:26:21.600
This storehouse, as you see, it's very close to the sea and during storms the sea salt gets up in the air.

00:26:20.760 --> 00:26:28.240
So the salty air from the sea blows through these gaps in the timber walls.

00:26:27.960 --> 00:26:29.080
It blows through, yes.

00:26:28.160 --> 00:26:31.520
This storehouse is from around 1600.

00:26:30.640 --> 00:26:37.000
There were no refrigerators in those times, so it's a way to preserve the meat.

00:26:36.200 --> 00:26:37.480
It's the only way.

00:26:38.000 --> 00:26:47.040
The skerpikjot can hang for up to six months in these little houses, curing in the salt-rich sea air.

00:26:46.200 --> 00:26:51.480
Salt draws out moisture, drying the mutton.

00:26:50.520 --> 00:26:58.960
It also prevents the growth of mould and microbes that can turn the meat rancid, making it safe for us to eat.

00:26:59.520 --> 00:27:02.600
But it doesn't do much for the smell.

00:27:01.720 --> 00:27:07.600
I really hesitate to ask, but may I try a very, very small piece?

00:27:06.640 --> 00:27:10.480
A very small piece.

00:27:09.880 --> 00:27:13.600
This is softer than this one.

00:27:15.160 --> 00:27:16.480
Yeah.

00:27:15.680 --> 00:27:19.080
These soft ones - hanging for around four, maybe five months.

00:27:19.040 --> 00:27:22.880
And these are harder - seven to eight months.

00:27:22.000 --> 00:27:25.480
I think I'll start with a soft one.

00:27:24.640 --> 00:27:26.760
A small piece.

00:27:27.120 --> 00:27:29.880
And do you eat it raw?

00:27:28.960 --> 00:27:30.080
Yes.

00:27:29.120 --> 00:27:32.960
Just straight in, this hasn't been cooked?

00:27:35.120 --> 00:27:37.920
Sometimes...

00:27:37.080 --> 00:27:38.200
No, no.

00:27:37.280 --> 00:27:41.920
This is just wind dried.

00:27:41.520 --> 00:27:42.680
You manage.

00:27:46.480 --> 00:27:47.600
Mm.

00:27:46.640 --> 00:27:49.760
You get used to it!

00:27:48.800 --> 00:27:50.840
I can see you might get used to it.

00:27:50.240 --> 00:27:53.840
Yes.

00:27:53.360 --> 00:27:54.480
It's, um...

00:27:53.840 --> 00:28:05.920
It has two consistencies - the outside that's been dried in the wind, which is quite tough, and the inside that's quite soft and like soft cheese.

00:28:06.120 --> 00:28:07.200
Yes.

00:28:06.480 --> 00:28:12.480
But the aroma in your mouth is fantastically powerful.

00:28:11.800 --> 00:28:13.200
It's very powerful, yeah.

00:28:12.320 --> 00:28:13.520
Yeah.

00:28:12.560 --> 00:28:14.800
It really goes around your whole mouth.

00:28:13.840 --> 00:28:15.880
Yeah.

00:28:15.200 --> 00:28:16.520
It goes up the back of your brain...

00:28:15.680 --> 00:28:17.200
Yes.

00:28:16.240 --> 00:28:20.600
..Round the back of your head and back down.

00:28:24.800 --> 00:28:39.080
Skerpikjot might be an acquired taste, but curing and salting meat in remote places like this, surrounded by an often angry sea, provides a lifeline.

00:28:38.320 --> 00:28:49.480
The isolation of these islands has created a hardy people, who, past and present, have used all their resources to survive.

00:28:57.640 --> 00:29:02.480
Food is not the only bounty provided by our seas.

00:29:14.240 --> 00:29:23.640
Artists have long looked to the changing moods of our coast for inspiration.

00:29:25.840 --> 00:29:36.600
From the rousing music of Vaughan Williams and the epic art of Constable, to Coleridge's Rime Of The Ancient Mariner and Shakespeare's Tempest.

00:29:35.760 --> 00:29:41.440
And hidden beneath the waves, nature forms its own art.

00:29:42.360 --> 00:29:48.400
An unexpected bounty that fires the imagination.

00:29:47.560 --> 00:29:51.400
It's just a little harder to see.

00:29:51.120 --> 00:30:06.840
On Devon's south coast, Hermione's peering into the deep to uncover one of the most fundamental and beautiful bounties within our seas.

00:30:07.000 --> 00:30:14.120
Torquay, a resort built and invented by the Victorians.

00:30:13.640 --> 00:30:28.280
150 years ago, people weren't just promenading up and down the piers, they were making the most of the mild climate and the sheltered coves in an altogether different way.

00:30:27.600 --> 00:30:36.440
For many Victorians, Torquay was the home of marine zoology and botany.

00:30:35.520 --> 00:30:39.600
The English Riviera once teemed with bonneted and bespectacled naturalists.

00:30:38.800 --> 00:30:49.560
And with ready access to sandy beaches and plentiful rock pools, they were all prospecting for wonders from the sea.

00:30:49.640 --> 00:30:50.840
They were exploring a life aquatic.

00:30:52.840 --> 00:31:02.000
Now, inside here are some of the world's drifters, little algae and animals that float with the currents.

00:31:01.160 --> 00:31:07.920
The Victorians were captivated by the microscopic world of plankton.

00:31:07.040 --> 00:31:08.280
A collective of plants and animals that drift with our tides.

00:31:10.120 --> 00:31:17.320
Most of these tiny floaters are almost impossible to spot with the naked eye.

00:31:19.800 --> 00:31:24.680
They are no one species, shape or size.

00:31:23.920 --> 00:31:33.800
Found in vast numbers throughout the world's ocean, they underpin the marine food chain.

00:31:32.880 --> 00:31:44.040
But 150 years ago, how on Earth could the Victorians even see this minute bounty from the sea?

00:31:43.200 --> 00:31:48.320
I'm meeting historian Kate Williams to find out.

00:31:48.480 --> 00:31:53.320
The Victorians were completely obsessed by the microscope and particularly with plankton.

00:31:52.480 --> 00:32:01.840
Plankton opened up a whole new world of mystery, of the magic of nature, and also about all this incredible, beautiful detail they could see.

00:32:03.040 --> 00:32:07.160
Microscopes were first invented in the 16th century.

00:32:06.360 --> 00:32:12.440
But it was one Victorian man's obsession with them that introduced plankton to the masses.

00:32:11.760 --> 00:32:20.320
Amateur naturalist John Quekett was a microscope fanatic who hand made one at aged 15.

00:32:19.440 --> 00:32:28.040
His dream was to develop microscopes for professionals and hobbyists alike.

00:32:29.160 --> 00:32:38.480
Quekett was really leading the way in the idea of what you could see under the microscope, particularly with plankton - he was obsessed with plankton.

00:32:38.920 --> 00:32:41.760
So, where does plankton come into the story, then?

00:32:40.920 --> 00:32:42.120
Well, plankton is the number one focus of the story.

00:32:41.160 --> 00:32:53.960
He used plankton as essentially a kind of eye test for the microscope, so he could check whether the microscope's resolution, the magnification was correct.

00:32:53.120 --> 00:33:00.680
And one kind of plankton, known as diatoms, would forever be the microscopist's friend.

00:32:59.920 --> 00:33:05.680
Because at every stage of magnification their cells reveal new details.

00:33:08.240 --> 00:33:11.080
So much so that they're still used today to check microscope resolution.

00:33:10.200 --> 00:33:11.320
As technology advanced, so Quekett's obsession grew.

00:33:10.680 --> 00:33:19.400
He wrote a book which sparked a passion for microscopy and plankton amongst the middle classes.

00:33:21.400 --> 00:33:30.040
The Practical Treatise On The Use Of The Microscope, not really a catchy title.

00:33:29.200 --> 00:33:30.440
It's a bestseller!

00:33:29.520 --> 00:33:33.440
No, it is a catchy title, this was a best seller!

00:33:32.600 --> 00:33:34.920
So, this...

00:33:35.000 --> 00:33:39.920
If we were two Victorian ladies, I'd say, "Miss Hermione, give it to me, I want it!"

00:33:38.960 --> 00:33:40.200
Because it was so fascinating.

00:33:39.240 --> 00:33:47.080
He persuaded your average middle class person that looking down a microscope was the most fun you could possibly have.

00:33:46.200 --> 00:33:55.120
"The microscope is the most important instrument ever yet "bestowed by art on the investigator of nature."

00:33:54.200 --> 00:34:12.040
Microscopes flew off the shelves into the parlours of the Victorian middle classes, but they didn't just look at plankton, they arranged it into astonishing displays to show to their friends.

00:34:13.159 --> 00:34:17.360
This is a rosette slide.

00:34:17.639 --> 00:34:23.960
Each individual element of the picture is a single piece of plankton.

00:34:23.120 --> 00:34:28.600
Dazzled by nature, the Victorians had created a new art form, one that you had to gaze down a microscope to admire.

00:34:29.760 --> 00:34:36.199
But how do you manipulate something so tiny into such stunning arrangements?

00:34:36.480 --> 00:34:43.960
One man keeping this Victorian craft alive today is plankton arranger Klaus Kemp.

00:34:43.040 --> 00:34:50.199
Klaus, plankton are so tiny, how did the Victorians go about picking them up.

00:34:52.760 --> 00:34:57.600
They were using a thing called a pig's eyelash.

00:34:56.840 --> 00:35:13.160
A pig's eyelash is a long stem with a slightly...arrowhead to it, which allows them to individually pick up a diatom and place it into position.

00:35:12.320 --> 00:35:21.320
Instead of a pig's eyelash, Klaus uses a fine glass needle to pick up individual diatoms to place in his artworks.

00:35:24.760 --> 00:35:28.320
I'm desperate to have a go to see if I can pick up a plankton.

00:35:30.440 --> 00:35:31.840
Oh, wow.

00:35:31.080 --> 00:35:35.040
It's just a slide covered in little...

00:35:34.280 --> 00:35:36.040
Oh, gosh!

00:35:35.440 --> 00:35:38.560
..Little, tiny fragments.

00:35:37.600 --> 00:35:39.160
It's a bit like looking at a slide of glitter.

00:35:39.760 --> 00:35:42.720
OK, I've got the needle positioned over one.

00:35:41.880 --> 00:35:43.000
Right, now drop the needle to touch the diatom.

00:35:43.920 --> 00:35:47.160
Keep going, keep going.

00:35:46.880 --> 00:35:48.360
You're going to clear the slide.

00:35:49.680 --> 00:35:51.800
Right, OK.

00:35:51.400 --> 00:35:52.600
Then move the slide out of the way.

00:35:51.720 --> 00:35:53.120
Klaus, I think it's fallen off.

00:35:56.320 --> 00:35:57.520
HE GASPS What?!

00:35:56.680 --> 00:35:57.920
I think it's fallen off.

00:35:57.040 --> 00:35:59.240
You're not supposed to make it fall off.

00:36:01.200 --> 00:36:04.720
Klaus, I just don't know how you do this all day.

00:36:03.760 --> 00:36:06.800
It's absolutely impossible.

00:36:06.520 --> 00:36:14.400
Klaus has taken this Victorian art form into the 21st century with his own unique style.

00:36:13.800 --> 00:36:29.760
The Victorians and their microscopes opened up the minute world of plankton, but they were only beginning to understand this bounty's full importance.

00:36:29.080 --> 00:36:41.440
Today, we know that plankton is not only fundamental to life in our seas, but to life on dry land, too.

00:36:40.840 --> 00:36:44.520
It emits half of all the oxygen we breathe.

00:36:43.720 --> 00:36:48.960
So, without plankton there would be no fish in the sea and probably no you and no me.

00:36:48.360 --> 00:36:56.360
Plankton underpin the very existence of every living thing on Earth.

00:36:55.440 --> 00:37:01.680
And with climate change affecting our seas, it's more important than ever to know what's happening to it.

00:37:03.440 --> 00:37:19.080
Marine biologist Richard Kirby is on a mission to measure plankton levels around the world, using nothing more than a secchi disc, which looks a lot like a dinner plate on a piece of string.

00:37:18.160 --> 00:37:24.080
You're measuring the depth at which it just disappears from sight beneath the surface.

00:37:24.000 --> 00:37:27.000
The disappearing disc allows Richard to measure the density of plankton in the water.

00:37:28.960 --> 00:37:37.760
So, as the disc disappears, the only thing that's preventing us from seeing it is the phytoplankton in the water?

00:37:37.000 --> 00:37:41.400
The creatures that start the whole marine food chain.

00:37:40.880 --> 00:37:42.600
It's just disappearing out of sight now.

00:37:41.800 --> 00:37:44.240
And the depth we're at is 10.4 metres.

00:37:46.080 --> 00:38:00.680
But Richard is just one man and our oceans are vast, so he's enlisting the help of sailors around the world to get their own secchi discs and submit their data to his survey via a mobile phone app.

00:37:59.760 --> 00:38:00.920
Got the app on.

00:38:00.080 --> 00:38:01.880
Secchi.

00:38:04.080 --> 00:38:05.440
And now we can input our depth.

00:38:04.480 --> 00:38:07.760
And we measured 10.4 metres, spot on.

00:38:07.680 --> 00:38:10.360
And submit.

00:38:11.000 --> 00:38:20.240
Richard's work, like that of the Victorian John Quekett, is putting these tiny creatures on the map.

00:38:19.440 --> 00:38:29.440
By engaging seafarers with plankton, he's safeguarding the future of this vital bounty from our seas.

00:38:46.360 --> 00:38:54.280
I'm on an overseas treasure hunt.

00:38:53.520 --> 00:38:55.000
My prize?

00:38:54.200 --> 00:38:58.960
Bounty from the sea.

00:38:58.120 --> 00:39:03.480
The Faroe Islands have been dubbed nature's larder.

00:39:02.520 --> 00:39:07.560
I'm on a journey to discover why plentiful seas make for rich pickings.

00:39:06.800 --> 00:39:14.400
But when foul tides bring hard times, you need a back-up.

00:39:15.880 --> 00:39:23.000
And for the Faroese, that bounty is farmed Atlantic salmon, their biggest export.

00:39:23.720 --> 00:39:31.160
There are 25 salmon farms in the Faroes, one in almost every fjord.

00:39:30.320 --> 00:39:41.800
They produce 70,000 tonnes of these slippery slabs of silver every year, worth a whopping £265 million.

00:39:40.880 --> 00:39:46.120
They're the number one foreign supplier of salmon into the UK.

00:39:45.840 --> 00:39:55.320
But keeping salmon contained in large numbers like this doesn't come without complications.

00:39:54.440 --> 00:40:03.920
The biggest threat to farmed salmon around the world is sea lice, a marine parasite.

00:40:03.040 --> 00:40:09.360
These copepods are a member of the crustacean family, like crabs and lobsters.

00:40:08.600 --> 00:40:15.320
Problematic for captive salmon, sea lice also target passing wild fish.

00:40:15.560 --> 00:40:20.640
But here in this tiny archipelago, they believe they could have a global solution.

00:40:19.840 --> 00:40:21.920
Welcome on-board.

00:40:21.720 --> 00:40:22.920
Thank you very much.

00:40:22.000 --> 00:40:30.200
Arni Olsen works for the biggest producer of salmon in the Faroes and they're exploiting their unique location.

00:40:30.160 --> 00:40:40.200
We're pioneers when it comes to farming in rough waters, because it's probably the farming area where you farm salmon with the strongest currents in the world.

00:40:39.600 --> 00:40:42.320
Doesn't it make it difficult for farming?

00:40:41.360 --> 00:40:47.040
It does make it difficult, but the current optimises the welfare of the salmon.

00:40:46.080 --> 00:40:49.040
We don't see the same kind of problems.

00:40:48.200 --> 00:40:53.200
For example, the sea lice numbers are much lower than in comparatively weaker currents.

00:40:52.480 --> 00:41:02.320
Farming in fjords with strong currents can stop sea lice taking hold, but doesn't eradicate them completely.

00:41:01.760 --> 00:41:09.680
To do that, the Faroese want to move their commercial treasure offshore, into the open ocean.

00:41:08.840 --> 00:41:16.920
But colossal seas present mountainous challenges.

00:41:16.160 --> 00:41:25.720
Marine scientist Oystein Patursson is overseeing trials that could make this ambition a reality.

00:41:25.320 --> 00:41:26.760
This was deployed on our test site in the open ocean.

00:41:26.480 --> 00:41:29.560
Is this a standard mooring line?

00:41:28.760 --> 00:41:36.240
No, these are more heavy-duty than would be used on a fish farm.

00:41:35.560 --> 00:41:41.360
And what's the breaking strain on this?

00:41:40.400 --> 00:41:44.520
So, it will hold 100 tonnes of force?

00:41:43.560 --> 00:41:45.000
Yes, yes.

00:41:44.240 --> 00:41:46.320
There would be 12 of these.

00:41:45.600 --> 00:41:49.120
That gives you...

00:41:48.160 --> 00:41:53.680
That's an incredible insight into the power of the ocean.

00:41:52.840 --> 00:42:00.080
Sea pens are tethered by a complex mooring system of heavy-duty ropes and chains.

00:41:59.200 --> 00:42:07.560
But there's more to open sea farming than simply holding the rigs in place.

00:42:06.680 --> 00:42:08.120
This is all broken.

00:42:07.400 --> 00:42:10.520
Yeah, you see the steel is broken here.

00:42:09.720 --> 00:42:11.000
That's amazing!

00:42:10.160 --> 00:42:14.040
This has been snapped by the power of the ocean?

00:42:13.120 --> 00:42:15.240
Yeah, just where the...

00:42:14.320 --> 00:42:15.480
Twist and twist...

00:42:14.520 --> 00:42:20.760
When the waves are moving by, it keeps on twisting and twisting.

00:42:19.880 --> 00:42:21.520
The forces must be fantastic.

00:42:20.920 --> 00:42:26.120
Yeah, we had very large waves during this winter.

00:42:25.560 --> 00:42:30.920
It was 16 or 18 metres and, er...

00:42:30.120 --> 00:42:31.560
16 or 18 metres?

00:42:30.720 --> 00:42:32.480
That's taller than a house!

00:42:31.600 --> 00:42:33.560
Yeah.

00:42:32.600 --> 00:42:40.920
The pens need to be strong enough to contain the fish, yet flexible enough to roll with wild waters.

00:42:40.080 --> 00:42:45.400
Trials are ongoing to perfect this challenging design.

00:42:47.600 --> 00:42:57.240
By working with all nature can throw at them, the Faroese could be the first to solve the sea lice problem.

00:42:56.320 --> 00:43:03.480
If they succeed, they'll be enhancing their own bounty and that of salmon farmers around the world.

00:43:02.720 --> 00:43:06.120
Making the most of their seas comes naturally to the Faroese people.

00:43:05.800 --> 00:43:13.800
This resourcefulness was a life saver in World War II.

00:43:26.360 --> 00:43:40.080
With Britain short of food, Faroese fishermen came to our aid, landing much-needed bounty at Aberdeen.

00:43:39.120 --> 00:43:44.560
The North Sea was awash with mines, German U-boats lurking.

00:43:43.680 --> 00:43:52.160
And to the south the Battle of the Atlantic raged, to keep food and supplies flowing.

00:43:51.520 --> 00:44:03.480
From a quiet corner of Cornwall, Tess is investigating how Britain's defence of this precious trade route, bringing in vital bounty from the sea, was launched.

00:44:04.920 --> 00:44:23.120
This coastline looks peaceful enough now, but during World War II, the Lizard Peninsula was home to a crack Air Force squadron, flying the deadliest, fastest aircraft, the Mosquito.

00:44:23.200 --> 00:44:31.640
This miraculous aircraft was a product of war, built with the sole purpose of taking on the Germans.

00:44:30.760 --> 00:44:37.120
It was the brainchild of aviation pioneer Geoffrey de Havilland.

00:44:36.280 --> 00:44:44.480
In an age of sluggish metal-clad bombers, he broke with convention - he made this one out of wood.

00:44:43.880 --> 00:44:49.240
This is a piece of an actual De Havilland Mosquito.

00:44:48.400 --> 00:44:52.800
It was part of the fuselage fished out of the Thames after a crash.

00:44:52.040 --> 00:44:58.720
And if you look here, you can see the outer and inner ply, between which is balsa wood, which is incredibly light.

00:44:57.800 --> 00:45:02.560
This whole piece is featherweight.

00:45:01.600 --> 00:45:14.640
De Havilland's wooden aircraft might have seemed vulnerable, but it solved the problem of metal shortages to become our fastest and most manoeuvrable bomber.

00:45:13.720 --> 00:45:25.200
It was constructed like a giant model plane, built in two halves and literally stuck together with glue.

00:45:26.320 --> 00:45:31.520
But what was it like to fly one of these timber terrors?

00:45:30.720 --> 00:45:38.760
I'm meeting Flight Lieutenant Des Curtis, one of the last surviving Mosquito crew.

00:45:37.920 --> 00:45:45.240
He flew from an RAF base here on the Lizard Peninsula to fight in the Battle of the Atlantic.

00:45:44.360 --> 00:45:47.280
Des, what was it actually like to be in a Mosquito, to fly one?

00:45:47.760 --> 00:45:57.640
Oh, very exhilarating because it was the fastest aircraft at the time, it was the most versatile aircraft.

00:45:56.680 --> 00:46:07.040
People say that the Spitfire was the most beautiful aircraft, but anybody who's ever flown in a Mosquito will tell you that was the best aircraft they ever flew.

00:46:06.960 --> 00:46:19.000
During the war, German U-boats sank 15 million tonnes of Allied shipping as they sought to starve supplies and cripple our war effort.

00:46:18.160 --> 00:46:24.280
The Mosquito's mission was to take these deadly submarines on.

00:46:23.560 --> 00:46:37.760
Our job then was to use this very fast moving Mosquito to seek out German U-boats in the Bay of Biscay and attack them as they were going in and out of the U-boat pens.

00:46:37.640 --> 00:46:51.680
All this noise going on while you were diving at 30 degrees onto the sea, by the time you got near the water level you were doing nearly 400 miles an hour, which was the maximum speed of a Mosquito.

00:46:50.960 --> 00:47:00.560
This wooden wonder seemed set to win the Battle of the Atlantic, but it wasn't long before it, too, came under threat.

00:46:59.840 --> 00:47:09.360
As the German blockade tightened its grip, concerns grew that the one vital ingredient needed to make the Mosquito would be cut off.

00:47:08.440 --> 00:47:18.480
Balsa wood was perfect for the job - light, strong and just the right compression strength for the manufacturing of Mosquitos.

00:47:18.360 --> 00:47:24.520
Only one problem, it came from Ecuador.

00:47:23.560 --> 00:47:35.160
With a raw material from the far side of the world and nothing even remotely similar on home soil, the race was on to create a substitute.

00:47:34.400 --> 00:47:36.800
The answer would come from an unexpected source.

00:47:35.840 --> 00:47:47.160
A German scientist stepped in with a unique solution - he would save our skies with bounty from our seas.

00:47:46.400 --> 00:47:51.000
Seaweed.

00:47:50.200 --> 00:47:56.240
With or without the German blockade, this was one resource we had in abundance around our shores.

00:47:57.760 --> 00:48:04.400
But who would think of building an aircraft out of seaweed?

00:48:03.600 --> 00:48:09.520
Step forward this man, Peter Plesch, a chemist.

00:48:08.760 --> 00:48:16.040
He was tasked with finding a home-grown material to keep Mosquitos rolling off the production line.

00:48:15.120 --> 00:48:27.920
Peter was born in Frankfurt, but the rise of Nazism in the 1930s forced his Jewish family to uproot themselves and flee to England.

00:48:27.480 --> 00:48:32.320
This German scientist had come to the aid of Britain's war effort.

00:48:31.560 --> 00:48:36.480
To tell me more is Philippa Neilson, his granddaughter.

00:48:35.640 --> 00:48:36.880
What do you think was his inspiration as a child?

00:48:35.920 --> 00:48:38.440
Why did he become a scientist?

00:48:37.560 --> 00:48:50.120
Well, his father was a doctor, a medical doctor, and one of his patients was Albert Einstein, who went on to become a family friend.

00:48:49.160 --> 00:48:52.080
That's not any old scientist, is it?

00:48:51.240 --> 00:48:52.400
No.

00:48:51.440 --> 00:48:54.640
So, as a child, Einstein would be in and around the family home.

00:48:53.680 --> 00:49:00.080
And here he is, Einstein with that unmistakable brainy hair.

00:48:59.280 --> 00:49:05.000
And Peter, then, in his own right, becomes a really eminent professor eventually, doesn't he?

00:49:04.080 --> 00:49:08.200
Yes, he was a specialist in polymer chemistry.

00:49:07.400 --> 00:49:16.200
He went on to write over 160 papers himself and, in fact, he wrote the last one when he was aged 90.

00:49:15.400 --> 00:49:27.920
In 1942, Peter Plesch joined a company called Cefoil, who were exploring the potential of transforming seaweed into a solid material.

00:49:27.040 --> 00:49:34.920
I've actually got a recording here of Peter talking about his first encounter with seaweed.

00:49:34.160 --> 00:49:49.160
Somebody at the Ministry of Aircraft production saw this very light and flimsy foam and said, "If you can make this material up to a certain standard "of mechanical strength, then we would be interested in this."

00:49:52.440 --> 00:50:13.400
So, the professor buried himself away in his laboratory, calculating and concocting experiments using alginates, a substance derived from seaweed, in a bid to create a substitute for the balsa wood used in the Mosquito.

00:50:13.160 --> 00:50:17.040
His work was top secret.

00:50:16.080 --> 00:50:22.240
This is a letter between staff members at the Ministry of Supply and it refers to the company Peter worked for.

00:50:21.760 --> 00:50:34.680
It reads, "MAP," Ministry of Aircraft Production, "are contemplating starting a production unit for the manufacture "of a solid foam from alginates "as a substitute for balsa wood for aircraft."

00:50:33.880 --> 00:50:42.680
"Experiments are understood to have been successful "and if the war goes on, this requirement may grow in importance."

00:50:41.800 --> 00:50:54.160
Peter Plesch turned seaweed into this material, which is as light and as strong as balsa wood.

00:50:53.200 --> 00:50:54.680
So, how did he do it?

00:50:55.840 --> 00:51:06.880
This is the last surviving piece of Peter Plesch's seaweed substitute suitable for the Mosquito.

00:51:06.880 --> 00:51:12.840
But the only evidence for how he created it lies in complex scientific equations.

00:51:13.960 --> 00:51:25.880
To us mere mortals, Peter's research is as clear as mud, so in order to crack the code I've enlisted the help of a top notch scientist and, together, we're going to conduct our own seaweed experiment.

00:51:28.880 --> 00:51:36.320
Dr Katherine Haxton is from Keele University.

00:51:35.640 --> 00:51:40.360
She's brought along some powdered alginate, extracted from seaweed.

00:51:39.440 --> 00:51:41.520
This is what Peter would have had to work with.

00:51:40.800 --> 00:51:46.680
OK, so we've got our solid alginate, and then we add the water to it.

00:51:48.520 --> 00:51:52.320
Alginate doesn't dissolve readily, so it clumps together...

00:51:52.400 --> 00:51:53.720
It's clotted.

00:51:52.800 --> 00:51:54.040
So, can I have a go?

00:51:53.080 --> 00:52:06.080
'With a bit of a blend and some foaming agent to add air bubbles 'to the material and make it light, 'we have a thick, sticky, gel-like substance.'

00:52:05.280 --> 00:52:06.640
It's kind of like crystals, almost, isn't it?

00:52:06.440 --> 00:52:07.600
It's quite fibrous.

00:52:06.640 --> 00:52:08.120
Yeah.

00:52:12.360 --> 00:52:18.200
'But this is still a long way from Peter's solid block.

00:52:17.480 --> 00:52:20.960
'So now we need to dry it.'

00:52:20.400 --> 00:52:27.360
So on his scale, to make, say, eight tonnes of alginate, he would have to remove 92 tonnes of water.

00:52:26.560 --> 00:52:30.760
That's about 1,000 bath tubs.

00:52:29.840 --> 00:52:36.920
'To do this, Peter turned to an innovative but dangerous method of 'using electricity and water to dry his material from the inside out.'

00:52:41.720 --> 00:52:46.920
Katherine, I was looking forward to doing this... ..but, well, nothing's really happening, is it?

00:52:47.920 --> 00:52:54.560
No, we wouldn't necessarily expect to see anything happening, but that doesn't mean that nothing's happening on a chemical basis.

00:52:53.600 --> 00:53:01.280
The modern equivalent, or what a 21st-century scientist might have reached for, would be a microwave.

00:53:00.360 --> 00:53:01.800
So, last stop is the microwave.

00:53:01.040 --> 00:53:07.440
Yeah, we'll put it in a microwave and start it drying.

00:53:07.040 --> 00:53:08.120
Great.

00:53:08.960 --> 00:53:13.400
The microwave may speed things up, but this could still take a long time.

00:53:15.040 --> 00:53:19.360
I do have a gel that I tried making earlier in the lab, which I can show you.

00:53:18.560 --> 00:53:19.800
OK.

00:53:18.840 --> 00:53:21.440
This was microwaved for about an hour.

00:53:20.800 --> 00:53:22.000
Right.

00:53:21.040 --> 00:53:24.400
Then it was dried in an oven.

00:53:26.800 --> 00:53:29.880
If you turn it over, you can start to see a sort of gel-like structure with bubbles in it to indicate the foamy nature.

00:53:31.000 --> 00:53:37.280
It's incredible to think he came up with this idea and worked it and worked it and worked it and made it happen.

00:53:36.320 --> 00:53:40.520
Yeah, it's no insignificant effort.

00:53:39.800 --> 00:53:45.320
It took a lot of ingenuity, a lot of perseverance, to get to the stage he did with the alginates.

00:53:45.400 --> 00:53:49.200
And, Katherine, he didn't have a microwave.

00:53:48.240 --> 00:53:49.360
No.

00:53:51.400 --> 00:53:58.160
Even with 21st century know-how, we failed to replicate Peter's work, a testament to his inventiveness.

00:53:57.240 --> 00:54:10.280
The ingenious German professor had transformed a bounty from the sea into a balsa wood replacement destined for the skies.

00:54:09.400 --> 00:54:17.600
So, why didn't we see a fleet of seaweed Mosquitos take on the German military?

00:54:16.640 --> 00:54:19.280
I'll let Peter Plesch tell you that.

00:54:20.280 --> 00:54:23.560
By the summer of '44, I had solved the problem.

00:54:22.880 --> 00:54:37.200
I'd produced a material which was good enough for the De Havilland engineers, but by then the Battle of the Atlantic had turned and the supply of balsa wood was no longer a problem.

00:54:36.240 --> 00:54:38.720
So I solved the problem but not in time.

00:54:37.760 --> 00:54:42.480
If it had been necessary, there was a solution there.

00:54:41.720 --> 00:54:49.760
Using seaweed, an everyday bounty from our seas, Peter Plesch achieved the seemingly impossible.

00:54:49.040 --> 00:55:02.240
That it never took off was down to the endeavours of the Allied Forces, including the Mosquito pilots who gained the upper hand and brought the Battle of the Atlantic to a close.

00:55:04.360 --> 00:55:05.520
One of the strange side-effects of war is the way it can set imaginations free.

00:55:04.600 --> 00:55:22.600
In the desperate search for solutions, there can be extraordinary flashes of inspiration, transforming humble seaweed into this, an ingenious wartime bounty.

00:55:26.920 --> 00:55:33.680
I'm nearing the end of my Faroese odyssey.

00:55:32.800 --> 00:55:49.200
I've discovered how the bounty from these seas nourishes a unique people, sustaining and maintaining a way of life steeped in tradition, but one where both eyes are firmly on the future.

00:55:48.320 --> 00:55:54.240
More than anywhere else, these islands thrive on their bounty.

00:55:53.360 --> 00:56:04.400
I've reached my final destination, and having netted my catch on land and sea, all that's left to do is eat it.

00:56:06.520 --> 00:56:09.080
I'm heading to a traditional Faroese gathering.

00:56:08.200 --> 00:56:15.960
But rather than bring a bottle, it's bring your bounty.

00:56:22.440 --> 00:56:32.760
The setting for my feast is a spectacular natural cut in the coastline called a Gjogv, giving this place its name.

00:56:31.920 --> 00:56:46.640
Bounty of all descriptions has been landed here, including tradition, like this one, the chain dance.

00:56:48.680 --> 00:56:57.760
It was a medieval popular music craze that swept through Europe in the 1200s.

00:56:56.920 --> 00:57:03.160
Dances were a way to share dramatic stories and legends about ancient times.

00:57:02.240 --> 00:57:09.400
They soon spread with travelling sea trade and that's how they washed up here in the 13th century.

00:57:08.520 --> 00:57:18.360
Stranded in the middle of the North Atlantic, this is now the only place on Earth to still practice this tradition.

00:57:17.920 --> 00:57:21.320
This is storytelling with a real passion, which is just rising from the sea and the rock.

00:57:22.680 --> 00:57:31.040
With 70,000 verses, dances can go on all night.

00:57:30.160 --> 00:57:35.200
So a pit-stop for some fuel is a must!

00:57:34.400 --> 00:57:38.160
Time to share my Faroese treasures from the sea.

00:57:37.320 --> 00:57:42.960
Does the Faroese food help make a certain kind of character?

00:57:42.880 --> 00:57:44.720
Yes, of course, of course.

00:57:43.840 --> 00:57:49.560
Because Faroese food is our food and we eat a lot from the sea.

00:57:48.840 --> 00:57:52.120
We're also eating from hills.

00:57:51.360 --> 00:57:57.320
This food comes from around our islands and in our islands.

00:57:58.240 --> 00:58:02.280
Bounty from the sea has shaped these people, carving them out like their rocky coastline.

00:58:02.320 --> 00:58:08.720
The Faroese use everything the seas offer to survive.

00:58:07.920 --> 00:58:09.920
In nature's larder nothing is wasted.

00:58:18.040 --> 00:58:30.080
I've learned so much from Faroese Islanders - finding bounty on land and sea, making a few resources go a very long way.

00:58:29.200 --> 00:58:36.760
It might be tradition here on the Faroes but it's a lesson for the rest of the world.

00:58:35.800 --> 00:58:43.320
Our waters are rich with wondrous surprises.

00:58:42.480 --> 00:58:49.240
They inspire, sustain and connect communities across the world.

00:58:48.520 --> 00:58:53.120
Bounty from the sea defines a way of life that all coastal people share.

00:58:52.160 --> 00:58:53.280
Hello.

00:59:20.880 --> 00:59:44.520
So glad that you could join us this evening.

01:00:00.800 --> 01:00:18.200
Friday nights in... ..with just the BBC Two of us.
